---
import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";

const toc = [
  { label: "Modes", href: "#modes" },
  { label: "Plan mode", href: "#plan" },
  { label: "Where it applies", href: "#where" },
  { label: "Choosing a mode", href: "#choose" },
];
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Permission modes"
  description="Control when an agent may edit files and run commands without asking. Permission is a property of the run, not of the whole project."
  currentPath="/docs/permissions"
  eyebrow="The workspace"
  {toc}
>
  <section id="modes">
    <h2>The four modes</h2>
    <p>
      Every thread and automation records a permission mode. Modesto passes that
      policy into the provider for the turn. The composer labels:
    </p>
    <div class="docs-table">
      <div>
        <strong>Supervised</strong>
        <span>
          Ask before commands and file changes. Use this when you want to watch
          every step, or when the repository is sensitive.
        </span>
      </div>
      <div>
        <strong>Auto-accept edits</strong>
        <span>
          File edits go through; other actions still ask. Useful when you trust
          the agent to write code but want to approve shell, network, or git.
        </span>
      </div>
      <div>
        <strong>Auto</strong>
        <span>
          Providers that support routine auto-approval take those actions;
          others still prompt. A middle ground when you are pairing with the run.
        </span>
      </div>
      <div>
        <strong>Full access</strong>
        <span>
          Commands and edits proceed without prompts. This is the default for a
          local desktop product. Use it when you are present and the tree is
          yours — not on an unattended automation unless you also set stop
          conditions.
        </span>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="docs-callout">
      <strong>Approvals do not expand access.</strong>
      <p>
        Saying yes to a command does not grant extra tools, extra directories, or
        merge rights. Repository permissions and provider account scopes still
        apply. Review does not get a higher mode than the thread that produced
        the diff.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="plan">
    <h2>Plan mode</h2>
    <p>
      Plan is an interaction mode, not a fifth permission. It asks the agent to
      propose a path without making the change. On Claude, Modesto maps Plan to
      the provider’s own plan permission; leaving Plan restores the thread’s
      base mode.
    </p>
    <p>
      Use Plan when the task is large, the repo is unfamiliar, or you want a
      checkpoint-quality brief before anyone edits. Then switch back and run
      the implementation on the same thread.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="where">
    <h2>Where the mode lives</h2>
    <div class="docs-table">
      <div><strong>Composer</strong><span>Set the mode for the next turn on the current thread.</span></div>
      <div><strong>Automations</strong><span>Each automation stores a snapshot so scheduled runs stay predictable.</span></div>
      <div><strong>Worktrees</strong><span>An isolated worktree limits blast radius even in full access — the agent still cannot silently rewrite your main checkout.</span></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="choose">
    <h2>Choosing a mode</h2>
    <div class="docs-steps">
      <article>
        <div>
          <h3>New or untrusted repository</h3>
          <p>Start Supervised. Widen only after you have seen how the agent behaves.</p>
        </div>
      </article>
      <article>
        <div>
          <h3>Routine implementation on your machine</h3>
          <p>Full access or auto-accept edits, plus a worktree if other work is in flight.</p>
        </div>
      </article>
      <article>
        <div>
          <h3>Unattended automation</h3>
          <p>Prefer a narrower mode, a worktree, iteration caps, and a stop condition. See Automations.</p>
        </div>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>
</DocsLayout>
